    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **28 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `locality` as a derived, read-only field on the owner, computed from the city via the fixed table (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, else `UNKNOWN`).\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema (alongside the other derived fields like `displayName`/`membershipTier`), so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes `locality` in every owner response.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/LocalityLookup.java`** (new) \u2014 A small static utility holding the fixed city\u2192region table and a `regionFor(city)` method returning the canonical region or `\"UNKNOWN\"`.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a MapStruct `@Mapping` expression for `locality` that calls `LocalityLookup.regionFor(owner.getCity())`, consistent with how the other purely-derived read fields are mapped.\n\nDeriving it in the mapper (rather than storing it) means it's correct for both the create response and subsequent `GET`s, matching the test flow.\n\n### One notable gotcha I hit and fixed\nMy first attempt put the lookup as a `default String localityFor(String)` method on the `@Mapper` interface. MapStruct treats **any** single-arg method on a mapper as a candidate mapping method, so it silently applied `localityFor(...)` to *every* String property (firstName, address, city, etc.), corrupting all fields and causing 500s. Moving the helper into a separate non-mapper class resolved it \u2014 that's why `LocalityLookup` is deliberately kept outside the mapper interface (documented in its Javadoc).",
